      "text": "To solve the infinite series \\(\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\frac{1}{x^{2^n} - x^{-2^n}}\\), we start by simplifying the general term of the series. The general term is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{x^{2^n} - x^{-2^n}}.\n\\]\n\nRewrite the denominator in a more convenient form:\n\n\\[\nx^{2^n} - x^{-2^n} = x^{2^n} - \\frac{1}{x^{2^n}} = x^{2^n} \\left( 1 - \\frac{1}{x^{2^n}} \\right) = x^{2^n} \\left( \\frac{x^{2^n} - 1}{x^{2^n}} \\right) = \\frac{x^{2^{n+1}} - 1}{x^{2^n}}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the general term simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{x^{2^n} - x^{-2^n}} = \\frac{x^{2^n}}{x^{2^{n+1}} - 1}.\n\\]\n\nNow, let's rewrite the series in a more compact form. Notice that:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^{2^n}}{x^{2^{n+1}} - 1} = \\frac{x^{2^n}}{(x^{2^n})^2 - 1} = \\frac{x^{2^n}}{(x^{2^n} - 1)(x^{2^n} + 1)}.\n\\]\n\nTo simplify further, consider partial fraction decomposition. We write:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^{2^n}}{(x^{2^n} - 1)(x^{2^n} + 1)} = \\frac{A}{x^{2^n} - 1} + \\frac{B}{x^{2^n} + 1}.\n\\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by the denominator \\((x^{2^n} - 1)(x^{2^n} + 1)\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nx^{2^n} = A(x^{2^n} + 1) + B(x^{2^n} - 1).\n\\]\n\nExpanding and combining like terms, we have:\n\n\\[\nx^{2^n} = (A + B)x^{2^n} + (A - B).\n\\]\n\nEquating the coefficients of \\(x^{2^n}\\) and the constant terms, we obtain the system of equations:\n\n\\[\nA + B = 1,\n\\]\n\\[\nA - B = 0.\n\\]\n\nSolving these equations, we find:\n\n\\[\nA = \\frac{1}{2}, \\quad B = \\frac{1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus, we can rewrite the general term as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^{2^n}}{(x^{2^n} - 1)(x^{2^n} + 1)} = \\frac{1/2}{x^{2^n} - 1} + \\frac{1/2}{x^{2^n} + 1} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{1}{x^{2^n} - 1} - \\frac{1}{x^{2^n} + 1} \\right).\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the infinite series becomes a telescoping series:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{1}{x^{2^n} - 1} - \\frac{1}{x^{2^n} + 1} \\right) \\right).\n\\]\n\nExpanding the series, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\left( \\frac{1}{x - 1} - \\frac